module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Constructions.SymmetricAlgebraSheafFunctorial
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Constructions.VectorBundle
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.NormalCone.ConormalSymmetricIso
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.NormalCone.NormalConeInNormalBundle

/-!
# The canonical morphism from the normal cone to the normal bundle is an isomorphism

Let `i : Z ⟶ X` be an immersion. [Stacks, Tag 0637] builds the canonical morphism
`C_ZX ⟶ N_ZX` from the normal cone to the normal bundle as the relative spectrum of the canonical
map of graded algebras `Sym*(𝒞_{Z/X}) ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X,*}` of [Stacks, Tag 0632]; that is
`Stacks.Conormal.normalConeToNormalBundle`. This file shows it is an **isomorphism** as soon as
that map of graded algebras is an isomorphism in every degree, and in particular for an effective
Cartier divisor.

The degreewise hypothesis is already proved: for an effective Cartier divisor the ideal is locally
generated by a nonzerodivisor, and then `Symⁿ(ℐ/ℐ²) ⟶ ℐⁿ/ℐⁿ⁺¹` is an isomorphism for every `n`
(`Stacks.Conormal.isIso_conormalSymmetricHom_app_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor`). What is added
here is the promotion of that degreewise statement to the schemes, in two steps.

The first is to invert the map of graded algebras itself, and that is already available:
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.inverse` takes the degreewise inverses and checks that
they are again multiplicative and unital, with the two composites identified in
`UnitalHom.comp_inverse` and `UnitalHom.inverse_comp`. Nothing below re-proves it.

The second is to push the resulting isomorphism through the relative spectrum. Functoriality of
the ungraded realization (`Stacks.RelativeSpec.gradedAlgebraSheafMap_id` and
`gradedAlgebraSheafMap_comp`) turns the two composites into the identities of the *algebra*
sheaves, so the induced morphism of algebra sheaves is an isomorphism; and the relative spectrum
of an isomorphism of algebra sheaves is an isomorphism by
`Stacks.RelativeSpec.isIso_relativeSpectrumMapOfAlgebraIso`, which the morphism of [Stacks,
Tag 0637] is identified with by
`Stacks.RelativeSpec.relativeSpectrumMapOfAlgebraHom_eq_ofAlgebraIso`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RelativeSpec.isIso_gradedAlgebraSheafMap`: **the morphism of algebra sheaves induced by
  a degreewise isomorphism is an isomorphism.**
* `Stacks.Conormal.isIso_conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom`: the canonical map of algebra sheaves
  `Sym(𝒞_{Z/X}) ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X,*}` is an isomorphism under the degreewise hypothesis.
* `Stacks.Conormal.isIso_normalConeToNormalBundle`: **the canonical morphism `C_ZX ⟶ N_ZX` is an
  isomorphism** under the degreewise hypothesis.
* `Stacks.Conormal.isIso_normalConeToNormalBundle_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor`: **for an
  effective Cartier divisor the canonical morphism `C_DX ⟶ N_DX` is an isomorphism.**

## Implementation notes

### Two spellings of the symmetric algebra sheaf

`Stacks.Conormal.normalBundleAlgebra` is `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.symmetricAlgebraSheaf`,
which is definitionally but not syntactically the `Stacks.RelativeSpec.gradedAlgebraSheaf` of the
symmetric graded algebra. Rewriting across that seam produces a goal that is not type-correct at
`implicit` transparency, so nothing below rewrites the morphism
`Stacks.Conormal.conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom`. Instead
`Stacks.RelativeSpec.isIso_gradedAlgebraSheafMap` is stated in the `gradedAlgebraSheaf` spelling
and applied to the `normalBundleAlgebra` spelling, which only asks the elaborator for
definitional equality of the two endpoints of a `Prop`.

For the same reason `Stacks.Conormal.isIso_normalConeToNormalBundle` goes through
`CategoryTheory.asIso` of the map of algebra sheaves rather than through a hand-built
`CategoryTheory.Iso`: the forward direction is then syntactically the morphism that
`Stacks.Conormal.normalConeToNormalBundle` is defined from, so identifying the two
relative-spectrum comparisons costs one delta step rather than a transport.

### No `@[stacks 0637]`

Tag 0637 asserts of `C_ZX ⟶ N_ZX` that it exists, that it is a closed immersion and that it is a
morphism of cones. The two clauses that are formalized are registered on
`Stacks.Conormal.normalConeToNormalBundle` and `normalConeToNormalBundle_toBase`. That the
morphism is an *isomorphism* for an effective Cartier divisor is none of the three: it is the
input the zero section of the normal line cone of [Stacks, Tag 0FEB] needs, and it is strictly
stronger than Tag 0637's closed-immersion clause while holding under a strictly stronger
hypothesis, so it neither is nor implies that clause. This follows
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.NormalCone.ConormalSymmetricIso`, which carries no
attribute for the same reason.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0630](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0630)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0632](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0632)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0636](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0636)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0637](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0637)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory Opposite TopologicalSpace

universe u

namespace Stacks.RelativeSpec

open SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra

/-- **The morphism of algebra sheaves induced by a unital homomorphism that is an isomorphism in
every degree is an isomorphism.** Its inverse is the morphism induced by the degreewise inverse
unital homomorphism `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.inverse`, and the two composites are
identities because the ungraded realization is functorial. -/
theorem isIso_gradedAlgebraSheafMap (X : Scheme.{u})
    {A B : SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.{u, u} X.ringCatSheaf}
    (f : UnitalHom A B) [∀ n, IsIso (f.app n)]
    (hcA : ∀ (p q : ℤ) (W : (Opens ↥X)ᵒᵖ) (a : (A.piece p).val.obj W)
      (b : (A.piece q).val.obj W), HEq ((A.mul p q).app W a b) ((A.mul q p).app W b a))
    (hcB : ∀ (p q : ℤ) (W : (Opens ↥X)ᵒᵖ) (a : (B.piece p).val.obj W)
      (b : (B.piece q).val.obj W), HEq ((B.mul p q).app W a b) ((B.mul q p).app W b a)) :
    IsIso (gradedAlgebraSheafMap X f hcA hcB) :=
  ⟨gradedAlgebraSheafMap X f.inverse hcB hcA, by
    rw [← gradedAlgebraSheafMap_comp, UnitalHom.comp_inverse, gradedAlgebraSheafMap_id], by
    rw [← gradedAlgebraSheafMap_comp, UnitalHom.inverse_comp, gradedAlgebraSheafMap_id]⟩

end Stacks.RelativeSpec

namespace Stacks.Conormal

variable {Z X : Scheme.{u}} (i : Z ⟶ X) [IsImmersion i]

/-- **The canonical map of algebra sheaves `Sym(𝒞_{Z/X}) ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X,*}` is an isomorphism** when the
canonical map of graded algebras of [Stacks, Tag 0632] is an isomorphism in every degree. This is
`Stacks.RelativeSpec.isIso_gradedAlgebraSheafMap`, the map of algebra sheaves being by definition
the one induced by `Stacks.Conormal.conormalSymmetricHom`. -/
theorem isIso_conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom
    (h : ∀ n, IsIso ((conormalSymmetricHom i).app n)) :
    IsIso (conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom i) :=
  haveI := h
  Stacks.RelativeSpec.isIso_gradedAlgebraSheafMap Z (conormalSymmetricHom i)
    (Scheme.Modules.symmetricAlgebra_mul_comm Z (conormalSheafDegreeOne i))
    (conormalAlgebra i).mul_comm_int

variable [hc : (conormalAlgebraSheaf i).IsQuasicoherent]

/-- **The canonical morphism `C_ZX ⟶ N_ZX` from the normal cone to the normal bundle is an
isomorphism** when the canonical map of graded algebras `Sym*(𝒞_{Z/X}) ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X,*}` of
[Stacks, Tag 0632] is an isomorphism in every degree.

The morphism is the relative spectrum of that map of *algebra* sheaves, which is an isomorphism
by `Stacks.Conormal.isIso_conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom`; the relative spectrum of an
isomorphism of algebra sheaves is an isomorphism by
`Stacks.RelativeSpec.isIso_relativeSpectrumMapOfAlgebraIso`, and the two relative-spectrum
comparisons agree by
`Stacks.RelativeSpec.relativeSpectrumMapOfAlgebraHom_eq_ofAlgebraIso`. -/
theorem isIso_normalConeToNormalBundle (h : ∀ n, IsIso ((conormalSymmetricHom i).app n)) :
    IsIso (normalConeToNormalBundle i) := by
  have := isIso_conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom i h
  have e : normalConeToNormalBundle i =
      Stacks.RelativeSpec.relativeSpectrumMapOfAlgebraIso (normalBundleSituation i)
        (conormalAlgebraSheaf i) hc (asIso (conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom i)) :=
    Stacks.RelativeSpec.relativeSpectrumMapOfAlgebraHom_eq_ofAlgebraIso
      (normalBundleSituation i) (conormalAlgebraSheaf i) hc
      (asIso (conormalSymmetricAlgebraSheafHom i))
  rw [e]
  exact Stacks.RelativeSpec.isIso_relativeSpectrumMapOfAlgebraIso _ _ _ _

/-! ### The case of an effective Cartier divisor -/

/-- **For an effective Cartier divisor `D ⊆ X` the canonical morphism `C_DX ⟶ N_DX` from the
normal cone to the normal bundle is an isomorphism.** The degreewise input is
`Stacks.Conormal.isIso_conormalSymmetricHom_app_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor`: the ideal of an
effective Cartier divisor is locally generated by a nonzerodivisor, so `Symⁿ(ℐ/ℐ²) ⟶ ℐⁿ/ℐⁿ⁺¹` is
an isomorphism in every degree.

Together with invertibility of `𝒞_{D/X,1}`
(`Stacks.Conormal.isInvertible_conormalSheafDegreeOne_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor`) this
presents the normal cone of an effective Cartier divisor as the total space of a line bundle on
`D`, which is what the zero section `o : D ⟶ C_DX` of [Stacks, Tag 0FEB] is taken along. -/
theorem isIso_normalConeToNormalBundle_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor {X : Scheme.{u}}
    (D : X.IdealSheafData) [Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor D]
    [Stacks.RelativeSpec.AlgebraSheaf.IsQuasicoherent (normalBundleAlgebra D.subschemeι)]
    [(conormalAlgebraSheaf D.subschemeι).IsQuasicoherent] :
    IsIso (normalConeToNormalBundle D.subschemeι) :=
  isIso_normalConeToNormalBundle D.subschemeι
    (isIso_conormalSymmetricHom_app_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor D)

end Stacks.Conormal

end
